### explain AX1001
AX1001 - unexpected character

The lexer found a character that cannot start any valid Axiom token.
An identifier is letters, digits, `_`, `'` and the operator characters
`+ - * / % < > = ! & | ^`, and cannot begin with a digit or `'`.
Operators are not a separate lexical class - `+` is an identifier - so
`set!`, `foo'` and `empty-list` are ordinary names. `?`, `~` and `@` are
not identifier characters at all, and `#` is one only as the `#|` that
opens a block comment. Check for typos, stray punctuation copied from
another language, or an unsupported Unicode character.

### explain AX1002
AX1002 - unterminated string literal

A `"` was opened but never closed before the end of input (or end of
line). Add a matching closing `"`, or escape an embedded `"` as `\"`.

### explain AX1003
AX1003 - unterminated character literal

A `'` character literal must contain exactly one character (or escape)
followed by a closing `'`, e.g. `'a'` or `'\n'`.

### explain AX1004
AX1004 - invalid number literal

The digits form a number literal that doesn't fit the expected numeric
format (e.g. it overflows i64/f64, or has a malformed exponent).

### explain AX1005
AX1005 - invalid escape sequence

Only `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\`, `\"`, `\'` and `\0` are recognized escape
sequences inside string and character literals.

### explain AX2001
AX2001 - unexpected token

The parser expected one kind of token (based on the surrounding
grammar) but found something else. This is usually a missing/extra
parenthesis, a misspelled keyword, or a declaration in the wrong
position.

### explain AX2002
AX2002 - unexpected end of file

The file ended while a form was still open, most commonly an
unbalanced `(`, `[` or `{`. Count opening vs. closing delimiters
working backward from the end of the file.

### explain AX2003
AX2003 - syntax error

A syntax rule was violated in a way that doesn't fit a more specific
diagnostic code; see the message for details.

### explain AX2004
AX2004 - removed language construct

This form was part of an earlier version of Axiom and no longer exists.
The keyword is still reserved so that old source gets this explanation
instead of being silently reinterpreted as an ordinary identifier.

`union` is gone because C interoperability is no longer a goal of the
language. An untagged union cannot be pattern-matched safely and has no
meaning under linear typing, where every value has exactly one owner and
one type. Use `data` for a tagged sum, whose variants the compiler can
check for exhaustiveness, or `struct` for a product.

`foreign` is gone because it never worked: a binding emitted a call to
a symbol the module never declared, so a program that used one passed
`check` and then died inside `opt` or the linker.

It is NOT the FFI under a new name, and the FFI is not a revival of it.
Axiom binds Rust through an `extern` BLOCK:

  (pub extern "axiom_demo"
    (add :: (-> Int Int Int) (symbol "axffi_add")))

built with `--link-lib` and `--link-search`. The emitter writes a
`declare` for each item, which is the entire difference. See
`docs/ffi.md`.

A program that declares no `extern` still links no C library at all,
and so does one bound to a `no_std` Rust crate - measured, `nm -u` on
both is empty. Reaching the kernel needs no FFI either: the standard
library is written in Axiom over `__syscall0`-`__syscall6`.

`region` is gone because memory is no longer scoped by an explicit
source-level block. Allocation lifetime is inferred: a value's arena is
determined by where it is created and how far it escapes, and it is
dropped deterministically at the end of that arena. Delete the `region`
wrapper; the body is the program.

`deriving` is refused rather than removed: the clause parsed and was
silently DISCARDED from the day it was written - its names never
reached the AST, no instance was ever derived, and a program carrying
one checked OK. A clause that does nothing is worse than one that does
not parse. Derive instances explicitly, where you want them:
`(deriveEq T)` is an ordinary declaration macro, and the instance it
generates is checked, compiled, and dispatched like a written one
(docs/macro-system.md MAC-CAP-9).

### explain AX2005
AX2005 - nesting too deep

A form nests more deeply than the parser's fixed limit. Every stage
after the parser walks the syntax tree recursively, so an unbounded
nesting depth is an unbounded native stack depth; the limit converts
what would otherwise be a stack overflow - a crash with no span and no
message - into this diagnostic, pointing at the token where the limit
was reached.

Hand-written code effectively never reaches the limit. Generated code
can: a long chain built by folding a binary operator over a list nests
once per element. Emit a `let` chain, a `{}` block, or a balanced tree
instead of one deeply nested form.

### explain AX3001
AX3001 - undefined variable

No binding with this name is in scope: not a local `let`/`lambda`
parameter, not a top-level `define`/`fn`, and not a data constructor.
Check for typos, missing imports, or a definition that appears later
in the file (Axiom does not forward-reference local bindings).

### explain AX3002
AX3002 - undefined type

This type name does not refer to any built-in type, `data`, `struct`,
or `type` alias visible in this module.

### explain AX3003
AX3003 - undefined constructor

This name was used as a data constructor (applied like `(Foo x y)` or
matched in a pattern) but no `data` declaration defines a constructor
with this name.

### explain AX3004
AX3004 - type mismatch

An expression's inferred type doesn't match the type required by its
context (a function parameter, `if` branch, `let` binding, etc.).

### explain AX3005
AX3005 - non-exhaustive pattern match

A `match` expression does not cover every possible constructor/value of
the scrutinee's type. Add the missing arms or a wildcard `_` arm.

### explain AX3006
AX3006 - duplicate definition

The same name is defined more than once at the same scope. Rename one
of the definitions or remove the duplicate.

### explain AX3007
AX3007 - field not found

The named field does not exist on the given `struct` type. Check the
field name for typos or that you're accessing the right type.

### explain AX3008
AX3008 - semantic error

A semantic rule was violated in a way that doesn't fit a more specific
diagnostic code; see the message for details.

### explain AX3009
AX3009 - constructor arity mismatch

A data constructor was applied (or matched in a pattern) with the wrong
number of arguments. Check the constructor's `data` declaration for how
many fields it actually has.

### explain AX3010
AX3010 - agent metadata mismatch

A `;@axiom:<key>(<value>)` tag on this declaration makes a claim
(e.g. `effect(io)`, `pure`) that the body does not support. The
compiler validates tags it can and reports mismatches so an agent
can correct the annotation instead of silently trusting it.

### explain AX3011
AX3011 - effect mismatch

The body of a `handle` expression performs effects that its
handled-effects list does not name. List every effect the body
can perform, or move the unhandled operation outside the
`handle`.

(Effects do not appear in function signatures; a function's
effect claims are made with `;@axiom:effect(...)` tags and
validated as AX3010.)

### explain AX3012
AX3012 - cannot assign to an immutable binding

`set` stores into a local, and a local is assignable only if it was
introduced as `(let ((mut x ...)) ...)`. Immutable is the default, so
a binding that is never reassigned needs no annotation and cannot be
changed by accident.

Add `mut` at the declaration, or - usually better - compute the value
you want with a `let` rather than mutating one you already have.

Only a whole local can be assigned. Function parameters, pattern
bindings and top-level declarations are never mutable, and structure
fields are written with `memSetWord` instead.

### explain AX3013
AX3013 - partial application is not supported

A top-level function was applied to fewer arguments than its
definition declares, or a function of two or more parameters was
used as a bare value. Both are well-typed under currying, but the
compiled representation has no way to hold the missing arguments:
a call site would simply omit them, and the callee would read
whatever happened to be in the argument registers - which is how
this used to run and crash instead of being reported.

Bind the missing arguments with a lambda instead: where `(f x)`
was meant, write `(lambda (y) (f x y))`. A lambda captures `x` in
a closure record, which is the representation partial application
would need and lambdas already have. Saturated calls, calls with
*extra* arguments applied to a returned function value, and
one-parameter functions used as values are all unaffected.

### explain AX3014
AX3014 - a bare name is defined in more than one imported module

Two or more imported modules define this name and the entry file
does not, so a bare reference has no principled winner - and before
this diagnostic the compiler's stages genuinely disagreed about
which definition it meant, so a program could type-check against
one module's signature and run the other module's code.

Qualify the reference as `Module::name` to pick explicitly, or
import only the names you want with `(import Module (name ...))`.
A name the entry file defines itself always resolves to the entry
file's definition and needs no qualification.

### explain AX3015
AX3015 - a signature with no definition behind it

A `(:: name Type)` signature declares a name; only a `(fn ...)`
gives it something to run. This name has a signature and no `fn` -
either the entry file declares the signature and never defines the
function, or an import delivered a `pub` signature whose definition
stayed private in its module.
Calling it used to pass `check` and then fail in the native
toolchain with an undefined-symbol error about generated code.

Add the definition, or mark the defining `fn` `pub` alongside its
signature so the import carries both.

### explain AX3016
AX3016 - a handle list names an effect that is not declared

The effects a `handle` expression may name are the built-ins
(`IO`, `Alloc`, `Mut`, `Div`) and effects introduced by an
`(effect Name (op :: sig) ...)` declaration in scope. This name
is neither, so the handle could never intercept or scope
anything - before this diagnostic it was accepted silently and
did nothing.

Declare the effect, import the module that declares it, or fix
the spelling.

### explain AX3017
AX3017 - an effect-handler shape the compiler does not support yet

Dynamic handlers currently support exactly one custom effect per
`handle` expression, and only effects that declare exactly one
operation; an effect operation is callable but cannot be used as
a bare value (there is no closure representation for dynamic
dispatch). Nest `handle` expressions to install handlers for
several effects at once, and split multi-operation effects into
one effect per operation until per-operation handler syntax
exists.

### explain AX3018
AX3018 - wrong number of arguments to a macro

A macro is applied to exactly as many arguments as its head declares
parameters. It is not a function and does not curry: an invocation is a
REWRITE, and the rewrite has nowhere to put an argument it was given no
parameter for.

Both mismatches used to be accepted. A missing argument left the
parameter's own name standing in the generated code, so
`(macro (two p q) (+ p q))` invoked as `(two 40)` emitted
`add i64 40, %q` and the native toolchain rejected it with an undefined
value - an error about generated code, carrying no span into the
source. A surplus argument was dropped WITHOUT being evaluated, so its
side effects silently did not happen.

Count the parameters and pass that many.

THE RULE FORM IS NOT ONLY ABOUT COUNTING, since MAC-LANG-15 made a
rule's parameters PATTERNS and MAC-LANG-18 made selection a match.
There the message reads

    no rule of macro `defN` matches this invocation;
    it accepts (defN (a b)) or (defN T 7)

and the invocation may well have passed a count some rule
declares - what failed is the SHAPE of an argument, or the value of
a literal the pattern spells out. The shapes listed are the rules'
own patterns as their author wrote them; a plain identifier in one
matches anything, and everything else has to be written as it
stands.

The code and its `macro-arity` slug are shared deliberately rather
than split, because MAC-LANG-18 specifies this generalisation by
name - "the arity diagnostic AX3018 generalised. Today's AX3018
becomes the one-rule case of it". The slug is narrower than the
condition it now carries, and that is the cost of keeping a stable
code the specification names. (The unreachable-RULE refusal went
the other way and took a new code, AX3033, because nothing
specified it sharing AX3020 - see that entry.)

### explain AX3019
AX3019 - macro expansion did not terminate

Expanding this macro reached the depth limit of 128. Expansion repeats
until no invocation is left, so a macro that always rewrites to an
invocation of itself - directly, or through a second macro that
rewrites back - never finishes.

Before the limit existed this crashed the compiler: `(macro (loopy x)
(loopy x))` segfaulted with no diagnostic and no output, in about 10 ms
of CPU time.

A macro cannot recurse on its arguments the way a function can, because
there is no test the expander can evaluate to stop. Write the recursion
as a function and let the macro expand to a call.

### explain AX3020
AX3020 - a macro declares the same parameter twice

Two parameters of this macro share a name, so a reference to that name
in the template has no single meaning. The expander resolves it to the
LAST argument, which means the earlier ones are accepted, evaluated in
source order, and then discarded - measured: `(macro (dup a a) (+ a a))`
invoked as `(dup 10 20)` answered 40.

Since MAC-LANG-15 a rule-form macro's parameters are PATTERNS, so
the binders this counts are every binder the patterns introduce,
nested ones included: `((m a (f a)) ...)` binds `a` twice and is
this code. `_` is exempt - it binds nothing, so several of them in
one rule are legal.

A rule that can never MATCH is a different thing and a different
code (AX3033). This one used to cover both.

Give each parameter a distinct name.

### explain AX3021
AX3021 - a macro template contains a form the expander cannot substitute into

The expander rebuilds a template node by node, replacing parameter
references with the arguments' syntax. This template contains a form it
has no case for, so it refused rather than emit the template's own
identifiers into the generated code.

This is a compiler limitation, not a mistake in the program, and it
exists to be loud. The eight silent miscompiles it replaced were all
the same bug: a form was added to the language and not to the expander,
whose default was to return the node unchanged - which compiled, and
produced code referring to names that existed only inside the macro.

Rewrite the template without the form, or add a case for it to
`substTpl` in `self_host/expand.ax`.

A DECLARATION template has the same code with a fixed list: it may
generate `fn`, `::`, `data`, `struct`, `type`, `effect` and `impl`
declarations, further macro invocations, and a `syntax/for` over
them. An `import` template and a nested `macro` refuse, at the
MACRO's own line rather than at an invocation, because a template
kind is a property of the macro. Both are refusals by DECISION
rather than by schedule: an `import` inside a template would reopen
module resolution, which has already run when declaration expansion
starts, and a nested `macro` would reopen the fixpoint that expands
it. A `trait` template does not reach this code at all - a trait
declaration inside a template is AX2003, from the parser.

This paragraph named `type`, `trait`, `effect` and `import` as the
four refusals until 2026-08-16. `type` and `effect` became template
kinds on 2026-08-15 and this list was not swept - which the sentence
after it gave away, naming a nested `macro` as one of "those four".

### explain AX3022
AX3022 - a macro parameter used as a `set` target was given an expression

`set` assigns to a binding, so its target has to be a name. Where a
template writes `(set p v)` and `p` is a macro parameter, the argument
passed for `p` has to be a plain identifier; this call passed an
expression, which names no binding to assign to.

Pass a variable for that parameter, or rewrite the template so the
parameter is not a `set` target.

### explain AX3023
AX3023 - a name its module does not export

The name exists. It is declared in another module, and that module
does not make it visible: either its declaration is not written `pub`,
or the `(import M (a b))` that brought the module in does not list it.

A module's declarations are all carried into the program - its own
bodies have to be able to call its own helpers - and `pub` decides
only which of them anything ELSE may name. Before that split the two
were one decision, and a private declaration was simply deleted: the
module's own calls to it became `AX3001 undefined variable` reported
at the library's own lines, in a file that merely imported it, and if
the importing program happened to define that name, the module's call
landed on the importer's definition instead - silently, at exit 0.

Write `pub` on the declaration, add the name to the import's list, or
call something the module does export.

### explain AX3024
AX3024 - macro expansion produced more than the limit

Expansion is a rewrite, so the program the compiler sees is the
expanded one, and this expansion produced a tree too deep or too large
to process. Two limits apply and the message says which was reached.

DEPTH is 1024 forms, the same number the parser refuses source at
(AX2005). It has to be checked separately because the parser measures
the SOURCE: a 500-deep template invoked 120 times nests 620 in the file
and 60,000 in the tree, and only the second number overflows the stack.
Before this limit that input crashed `check`, `run`, `symbols` and the
language server with no output at all.

SIZE is 2,000,000 forms, and no depth limit can see what it catches:
`(macro (m x) (+ x x))` doubles per level, so 26 nested invocations are
154 bytes of source and 2**26 forms. That took 41 seconds and the
language server never answered.

Both are far above any real macro - the whole of `stdlib/Pre.ax`
expands to depth 1. Make the macro produce less, or write the repeated
part as a function and let the macro expand to a call: a function is
compiled once however many times it is called, which is the whole
difference.

### explain AX3025
AX3025 - a trait implementation the compiler cannot use

A trait is a set of method signatures written against one type
parameter; an `impl` gives those methods a body for one concrete type.
The pair (trait, type) is what a call is dispatched on, so both halves
have to be there and have to agree. This code covers every way they do
not:

  - the `impl` names a trait that is not in scope
  - it defines a method the trait does not declare
  - it does not define one the trait does declare and the trait
    supplies no default body for it
  - the trait has no type parameter, so there is nothing to key an
    implementation on
  - a call has no implementation for the type of its dispatch argument
  - a method mentions the trait's parameter in no parameter position,
    so no argument can select an implementation - there is no
    return-type-directed dispatch

Dispatch is by the STATIC type of the argument at the call site, and it
is resolved at compile time: the call becomes a direct call to that
implementation, with no table and no indirection. A type variable is
not a type, so a function that is generic over the trait cannot yet
call its methods - see docs/self-hosting.md 22.

### explain AX3026
AX3026 - a name the emitted runtime owns

A compiled Axiom program contains its own runtime - the allocator, the
traps, the arena primitives, and since 2026-08-15 the reference-count
primitives - emitted into the module it is built from. `axiom_alloc`
is the allocator's symbol, and `axiom_retain`/`axiom_release` are the
ownership primitives' (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2b). The backend
emits all three definitions unconditionally, so a program defining
any of the names is two definitions of one symbol.

Before this refusal that program passed `check` and died in `opt`
with `invalid redefinition of function 'axiom_alloc'` - the
documented "override seam" that never worked. The seam does not
exist; whether it should is re-decided with the reclamation work.
See docs/memory-model.md MM-ALLOC-8.

Only an entry-file definition collides: a module's declaration is
mangled to `Mod$axiom_alloc`, a different symbol.

### explain AX3027
AX3027 - a declaration-macro invocation the expander cannot resolve

A top-level form whose head is not a declaration keyword is a
DECLARATION MACRO INVOCATION: the head must name a visible rule-form
macro - `(macro name ((name args ...) (fn ...) ...))` - whose
template declarations are instantiated in its place. This code covers
every way that fails:

  - the head names no visible macro (a typo'd keyword lands here too,
    where it used to be a bare `syntax error`)
  - the head names an EXPRESSION macro, whose template is one
    expression and cannot stand in declaration position
  - a declaration macro is referenced in EXPRESSION position - the
    two template kinds are disjoint
  - the invocation sits in a pipeline carrying no mangling records -
    a re-expansion of an already-resolved program - so a module-side
    invocation there could generate a declaration nothing can name
  - an argument bound to a NAME position of the template - a
    declaration head or a parameter list - is not a bare identifier

See docs/macro-system.md MAC-CAP-8.

### explain AX3028
AX3028 - a syntax/* query with no answer

The `syntax/` spellings are the compile-time QUERY VOCABULARY: a
closed, compiler-implemented set of questions a macro template asks
about the declaration list, answered at expansion time with no user
code running. The vocabulary answers `(syntax/join a b)` in a
declaration NAME position and, applied, as a REFERENCE to what such
a name declares - `((syntax/join show T) x)`; `(syntax/constructors
T)`, `(syntax/fields S)` and `(syntax/binders C p)` as iteration
sequences; `(syntax/for (x seq) ...)` where a match ARM, a template
DECLARATION, or a call ARGUMENT stands, over one sequence or
several zipped in lockstep as `((x xs) (y ys))`; `(syntax/fold f z binds tpl)`
as the right-fold over one sequence or several zipped in lockstep;
`(syntax/same f g)` over two iteration variables; and the three
SCALAR queries `(syntax/name x)`, `(syntax/arity C)` and
`(syntax/defined n)`, which answer one node where one value is
needed. This code covers every way a query has no answer:

  - a `syntax/` head the vocabulary does not implement, or one
    standing in a position it does not own - the vocabulary is
    CLOSED, so unknowns refuse by name instead of dying later as
    undefined variables; a sequence in a one-value position and a
    syntax/for at expression root land here too
  - a subject with nothing to answer: constructors of a struct,
    fields of a data type, binders or an arity of a name that is not
    a constructor, any of them of a name that declares nothing or of
    a type its module keeps private
  - a query ARGUMENT that is neither a bare identifier nor a
    `(syntax/join a b)` - the one query whose answer is itself a
    name, and therefore the only one that can feed another's
    argument
  - parallel sequences of different lengths, in a syntax/for or a
    syntax/fold alike - the zip rule is a diagnostic, never a
    truncation to the shorter
  - a parallel binding that is not a `(y ys)` pair
  - syntax/same over variables ranging over DIFFERENT sequences - a
    false there would be a wrong answer, not a comparison - or over
    a name no enclosing syntax/for binds
  - a query written OUTSIDE a macro template - a (syntax/join ...)
    name, a syntax/for arm, or a top-level syntax/for in ordinary
    code has no instantiation to resolve it
  - a declaration NAMED into the reserved `syntax/` prefix, including
    the near-miss `(fn (syntax/join a b) ...)` - one paren short of a
    joined name, which would otherwise silently declare a function
    called syntax/join

See docs/macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5 and MAC-CAP-6.

### explain AX3029
AX3029 - a declaration too wide for the reference map

Every heap block born of `axiom_alloc` carries a 16-byte header,
and the header's shape word packs three fields: the form bit, the
block's padded payload word count, and - for the record form - one
bit per payload word saying whether that word is a REFERENCE the
release path must follow (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d). Bits
16..62 carry the map; bit 63 is the i64 sign bit, reserved so every
shape constant the compiler emits is non-negative. That gives 47
mappable payload words: a struct may declare 47 fields, and a data
constructor 46 beside its tag word.

A declaration past the cliff is REFUSED at check time rather than
truncated at run time - a map that silently dropped its widest
field would free everything it named and leak the one it could
not. The largest declaration in this repository, the 37-field CG
record in the compiler's own code generator, fits with a quarter
of the map to spare; the cliff is far from real programs.

The escape hatch is structural: split the declaration. A nested
struct or constructor cell is ONE word of the outer block - the
walk follows the pointer, and the inner block carries its own map -
so any record too wide for one map becomes two that fit.

See docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d.

### explain AX3030
AX3030 - too many type variables for one evidence word

A polymorphic function receives one hidden machine word beside its
arguments: bit i says whether type parameter i is instantiated at a
REFERENCE type, and the reference maps of MM-LIFE-2d read those
bits where a declared type is a variable (docs/memory-model.md).
One word carries 64 bits, so a declaration may introduce at most 64
type variables.

The cap is soundness, not tidiness: a variable's bit is read with a
shift by its index, and a shift of 64 or more is poison in the
emitted LLVM - an arbitrary answer at run time, which under
reference maps becomes a wrong free. A sixty-fifth variable is
therefore refused at the declaration, on the merged declaration
list, imported modules included. The widest signature in this
repository declares 4.

Signatures whose every variable appears only in RETURN position
take no evidence word at all - no argument can ever witness one -
so the hottest accessors are exempt from the hidden argument
entirely; the cap still applies to the declaration's spelling.

See docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d.

### explain AX3031
AX3031 - malformed format string

A format string is parsed at EXPANSION time, by the compiler,
before the program is type-checked - so a hole that names nothing,
a brace that never closes, and a stray `}` are all compile errors
rather than runtime surprises. This is the whole reason the
printing macros exist: `(println "x = {x}")` is a rewrite into
`strConcat` and `show` calls, and there is no runtime format
parser to fail in.

The grammar is small on purpose:

  {name}   interpolate the binding `name`, in scope AT THE CALL
  {{       a literal `{`
  }}       a literal `}`

A hole holds a bare name and nothing else. There are no positional
holes (`{}`, `{0}`) and no argument list: capture is the form the
language has. A variadic `println` is buildable since a rule may
repeat (docs/macro-system.md MAC-LANG-16, 2026-08-16) and is
unbuilt; this paragraph said repetition did not exist until then.

A hole MAY carry a specifier, and the compiler picks the `Fmt` call
at expansion time rather than leaving it to you:

  {n:x}      (fmtHex n)
  {x:.2}     (fmtFloatPrec x 2)
  {n:>8}     (fmtPadLeft (show n) 8)
  {s:<12}    (fmtPadRight (show s) 12)
  {s:^12}    (fmtPadCenter (show s) 12)
  {n:04}     (fmtPadZerosLeft (show n) 4)
  {x:>10.2}  (fmtPadLeft (fmtFloatPrec x 2) 10)

This paragraph said there were no specifiers and told you to build
the padded or rounded string by hand with `fmtPadLeft`,
`fmtFloatPrec` or `fmtHex` - the calls the specifier now emits for
you. It said so until 2026-08-16, a day after they shipped.

A hole whose name is not in scope is AX3001, and one whose type
has no `Show` instance is AX3025; neither is this code. This code
is only for a string the parser could not make holes out of at
all.

See docs/macro-system.md MAC-CAP-10.

### explain AX3033
AX3033 - a macro rule that can never match

A rule-form macro's rules are tried IN ORDER and the first whose
pattern matches wins (docs/macro-system.md MAC-LANG-18). A rule
nothing can reach is therefore a program someone wrote expecting it
to run, and this code refuses it at the macro's own line rather than
letting it sit there looking live.

A rule is unreachable when an EARLIER rule of the same arity is
IRREFUTABLE - every one of its elements is a plain binder, so it
matches every invocation of that arity and consumes them all.

    (macro defU ((defU T)      ; matches any one argument
                 ...)
                ((defU (a b))  ; AX3033: nothing reaches it
                 ...))

Swap them and both are reachable: `(defU (a b))` fails on an
argument that is not a two-element form, and `(defU T)` then takes
everything else. Ordering a pattern table from most specific to most
general is the shape this diagnostic is steering toward.

Two rules of one arity are NOT refused by themselves. That was the
older rule, when arity was the whole selector and this condition
shared AX3020 with a repeated parameter; patterns made the two
different claims, so they became different codes. A macro whose
rules all take one argument and are told apart by shape is exactly
what the pattern language is for.

### explain AX3034
AX3034 - an ellipsis used at the wrong depth

A rule's last element may REPEAT: `(m T v ...)` takes one argument
and then any number, and `v` is bound to all of them at once
(docs/macro-system.md MAC-LANG-16). A name bound that way is many
forms, not one, and the template has to say where they go.

    (macro mk ((mk T v ...)
       (:: (syntax/join make T) T)
       (fn ((syntax/join make T)) (T v ...))))

    (mk Pair 1 2)      ; (Pair 1 2)
    (mk Trip 3 4 5)    ; (Trip 3 4 5)

Four ways to get the depth wrong, and this code is all of them:

  - the template names a repeating element with no `...` after it.
    That position takes ONE form and the name is many, so there is
    nothing to put there. Add `...`, or stop the element repeating.
  - `...` follows something that does not repeat - a fixed element,
    or nothing at all. There is no sequence to splice.
  - a rule declares more than one `...`. A repeating element absorbs
    every argument left after the fixed ones, so a second has nothing
    defined to take, and where the split fell would be the expander
    guessing.
  - a rule repeats a PATTERN rather than a bare name. v1 binds ONE
    name to the forms a repeat absorbs; repeating `(a b) ...` would
    bind `a` and `b` to a sequence each, in lockstep, which is a
    second feature and is not built.

The first two are reported at the invocation, the last two at the
macro's own line - the place each author can act on.

`...` is an ordinary identifier to the lexer, spelled with three
dots. That is why it stands beside the name it repeats rather than
gluing to it, and why a single `.` still means field access.

### explain AX3035
AX3035 - a macro parameter used as a binder was given something that is not a variable

A template may put a macro parameter in a BINDER position - a `let`
binding, a `lambda` parameter, or a pattern binder in a match arm:

    (macro (try! x e body)
      (match e
        ((Err er) (Err er))
        ((Ok x) body)))

    (try! v (parse s) (use v))

The parameter names the binding the CALLER wants, so the expansion
binds `v` and the caller's body reads `v`. That only works if the
argument is a name: handed `(try! (f 1) ...)` there is nothing to
bind, and the invocation is refused here rather than expanded into an
arm that binds an application.

This is the same rule `set` targets follow (AX3022), for the same
reason - a position that takes a NAME cannot take an expression, and
a macro parameter is the one way an expression can arrive in one.

A binder the TEMPLATE introduces is unaffected. It is renamed at every
expansion so it cannot capture a name the caller passed in, which is
the forward half of hygiene; a parameter in binder position is the
opposite case, where the caller named the binding and must be able to
read it (docs/macro-system.md MAC-HYG-10).

### explain AX4001
AX4001 - no `main` function

An executable Axiom program needs exactly one top-level function named
`main` to serve as the entry point. Add `(:: main Int)` and
`(define main ...)`, or use `axiom check`/`emit-llvm` if you only want
to analyze a library-style module.

### explain AX4002
AX4002 - code generation failed

The IR-to-LLVM lowering stage hit a case it doesn't yet support or an
internal invariant was violated. This generally indicates a compiler
limitation rather than a mistake in your source; please file a bug
with a minimal reproduction.

### explain AX4003
AX4003 - external toolchain command failed

Axiom shells out to `llc` and `cc` to turn LLVM IR into a native
executable. This error means one of those external commands could not
be run or exited unsuccessfully; check that LLVM (`llc`) and a C
compiler (`cc`) are installed and on your `PATH`.

### explain AX4004
AX4004 - an `extern` names a symbol no archive defines

An `extern` item binds a linker symbol, and the build checks - before
writing a byte or spawning a tool - that some archive on the link line
could define it. This error means none can.

Usually the `(symbol "...")` clause is a typo, or the archive that
defines it is not on the line: pass it with `--link-lib NAME` and
`--link-search DIR`, where DIR holds `libNAME.a`.

This is refused HERE, and that is the whole point. The removed
`foreign` construct emitted a call to a symbol its module never
declared, so a program using one passed `check` and then died inside
`opt` or the linker, with a message about generated code and no span
into the source. An `extern` naming a symbol that does not exist is the
same defect, and refusing it at the compiler rather than at the
toolchain is the difference between a diagnostic and a link error about
a file you never wrote.

What the check proves is one-directional: the name appears nowhere in
any archive on the link line, so nothing there can define it. It does
not claim the converse - a name that does appear might be a string
constant rather than a definition - because a false rejection would
break a correct build, while the case that actually happens is a typo.

It is a `build` diagnostic and cannot fire under `check`, which has no
link line to consult.

### explain AX5001
AX5001 - cannot resolve import

An `(import Mod.Sub ...)` declaration names a module path that doesn't
correspond to any file on disk. Module paths are resolved relative to
the entry file's own directory (the file passed to `check`/`build`/
`run`/`emit-llvm`), not the directory of whichever file wrote the
`(import ...)`: `Mod.Sub` looks for `Mod/Sub.ax` next to (or below)
the entry file, from every file in the program alike.

### explain ax3001 (exit 0)
AX3001 - undefined variable

No binding with this name is in scope: not a local `let`/`lambda`
parameter, not a top-level `define`/`fn`, and not a data constructor.
Check for typos, missing imports, or a definition that appears later
in the file (Axiom does not forward-reference local bindings).

### explain 3001 (exit 0)
AX3001 - undefined variable

No binding with this name is in scope: not a local `let`/`lambda`
parameter, not a top-level `define`/`fn`, and not a data constructor.
Check for typos, missing imports, or a definition that appears later
in the file (Axiom does not forward-reference local bindings).

### explain AX-3001 (exit 0)
AX3001 - undefined variable

No binding with this name is in scope: not a local `let`/`lambda`
parameter, not a top-level `define`/`fn`, and not a data constructor.
Check for typos, missing imports, or a definition that appears later
in the file (Axiom does not forward-reference local bindings).

